ruggle to afford housing. Median wages in <strong data-note=\"17\">17 of the 25 most common occupations<\/strong> in the United States — such as retail sales workers, building cleaners, nursing aides, and administrative assistants — are too low to enable a full-time worker to afford typical rents even for a one-bedroom unit.<\/p>", "state": {"highlight": ["working", "notworking"], "bracket": {"label": "34% total"}}}, {"html": "", "state": {}}]}, {"type": "prose", "heading": "Supply-Side Policies Can’t Make Rents Affordable to Lowest-Income People", "paragraphs": ["The problems described above all stem from the large gap between typical market rents and the amount that households with the lowest incomes can afford."]}, {"type": "scrolly", "figure": {"number": 4, "title": "Many of the Lowest-Income Households Can't Afford Rent Sufficient to Cover Housing Operating Costs", "notes": ["*Extremely low-income means up to the poverty line or 30 percent of the area median income, whichever is higher."], "source": "Source: CBPP analysis of 2024 American Community Survey data and 2024 Income/Expense IQ Benchmarking Report from National Apartment Association, Institute for Real Estate Management, and Building Owners and Management Association", "chart": {"type": "bar", "format": "usd", "valueLabel": "Monthly amount", "alt": "Bar chart: rent affordable to the median extremely low-income renter household is $308 a month; median overall rent is $1,487; median rental housing operating cost is $665.", "items": [{"key": "afford", "label": "Rent affordable to median extremely low-income renter household*", "value": 308, "color": "#7fb77e"}, {"key": "rent", "label": "Median overall rent", "value": 1487, "color": "#ffdc9a", "gapBefore": true}, {"key": "opcost", "label": "Median rental housing operating cost", "value": 665, "color": "#ffdc9a"}], "stackLabels": true}}, "steps": [{"html": "<p>In 2024, the median extremely low-income renter household had an annual income of $12,300, enough to afford <strong data-note=\"15\" data-guess=\"no endnote in this paragraph; the report’s chart of these figures cites CBPP analysis of 2024 American Community Survey data\">$308<\/strong> per month (30 percent of their income) in rent.<\/p>", "state": {"show": ["afford"], "strong": ["afford"], "refLines": [{"value": 308, "label": "Maximum rent extremely low-income renters can afford"}]}}, {"html": "<p>But the median rent nationally was close to five times higher, at <strong data-note=\"15\" data-guess=\"no endnote in this paragraph; the report’s chart of these figures cites CBPP analysis of 2024 American Community Survey data\">$1,487 per month<\/strong>.<\/p>", "state": {"show": ["afford", "rent"], "strong": ["rent"]}}, {"html": "<p>The median rental unit in a 2024 industry survey had a monthly operating cost of <strong data-note=\"23\">$665<\/strong>, almost double the $308 that the median extremely low-income household could afford.<\/p>", "state": {"show": ["afford", "rent", "opcost"], "highlight": ["afford", "opcost"], "strong": ["opcost"], "refLines": [{"value": 665, "label": "Minimum rent for landlords to break even", "side": "right"}]}}, {"html": "<p>No matter how many units are built or how much construction costs are subsidized, this won’t be enough to enable owners to reduce rents below the break-even amount needed to cover their operating costs.<\/p>", "state": {"show": ["afford", "rent", "opcost"]}}]}, {"type": "scrolly", "figure": {"number": 5, "title": "Homelessness Has Persisted as Market Swung Between Surplus and Shortage", "chart": {"type": "image", "src": "data:image/png;base64,", "alt": "Two bar charts, 2007 to 2025. Top: the number of people experiencing homelessness stayed between roughly 550,000 and 650,000 from 2007 to 2022 before rising to about 770,000 in 2024. Bottom: the housing market moved from a surplus of about 2 million units in 2007-2010 to a shortage of about 3 million units in 2021. Sources: Moody's Analytics, HUD.", "layers": [{"id": "topLater", "fade": true, "scrub": "up", "shapes": [{"maskImage": "data:image/png;base64,"}], "sequence": [[1086, 268, 42, 436], [1035, 268, 43, 436], [984, 268, 43, 436], [934, 268, 42, 436], [833, 268, 42, 436], [782, 268, 43, 436], [731, 268, 43, 436], [681, 268, 42, 436], [630, 268, 43, 436], [580, 268, 42, 436], [529, 268, 43, 436], [478, 268, 43, 436], [428, 268, 42, 436]]}, {"id": "botLater", "fade": true, "scrub": "down", "shapes": [{"maskImage": "data:image/png;base64,"}], "sequence": [[398, 898, 45, 414], [451, 898, 44, 414], [504, 898, 44, 414], [556, 898, 45, 414], [609, 898, 45, 414], [662, 898, 44, 414], [715, 898, 44, 414], [768, 898, 44, 414], [821, 898, 44, 414], [874, 898, 44, 414], [926, 898, 44, 414], [979, 898, 44, 414], [1032, 898, 44, 414], [1085, 898, 44, 414]]}], "width": 1160, "height": 1659}}, "steps": [{"html": "<p>This helps explain why high levels of housing-related hardship have <strong data-note=\"24,25\" data-guess=\"no endnote on this sentence; the next two sentences’ endnotes give the homelessness and worst-case-needs evidence\">persisted for decades<\/strong> even as the housing market has swung between shortages and surpluses.<\/p>", "state": {"view": {"y0": 0, "y1": 47}, "dim": [{"rect": [0, 795, 1160, 595]}], "layers": {"topLater": 0, "botLater": 1}}}, {"html": "<p>The number of people experiencing homelessness has <strong data-note=\"24\">never fallen below 550,000<\/strong> since HUD began its annual count in 2007, and it <strong data-note=\"24\">exceeded 620,000 from 2007 to 2011<\/strong> even though vacancy rates were at or near all-time highs.<\/p>", "state": {"view": {"y0": 0, "y1": 47}, "dim": [{"rect": [0, 795, 1160, 595]}], "layers": {"topLater": 1, "botLater": 1}}}, {"html": "", "state": {"view": {"y0": 46, "y1": 100}, "dim": [{"rect": [0, 170, 1160, 595]}], "layers": {"topLater": 1, "botLater": 1}}}, {"html": "<p>In short, if large numbers of people lack the resources to pay rent that’s sufficient to cover the cost of operating and maintaining housing, adding more units alone could make only <strong data-note=\"24,25\" data-guess=\"no endnote on this sentence; a conclusion drawn from the evidence in the paragraph before it\">a modest dent<\/strong> in problems like eviction and homelessness.<\/p>", "state": {"layers": {"topLater": 0, "botLater": 0}}}]}, {"type": "prose", "heading": "Expanding Rental Assistance Would Sharply Cut Homelessness and Hardship", "paragraphs": []}, {"type": "scrolly", "figure": {"number": 6, "title": "Rental Assistance Sharply Reduces Homelessness and Other Hardship", "notes": ["Note: The chart compares families with children who were randomly selected to receive vouchers and used them to families in a control group who did not use vouchers."], "source": "Sources: Effects of Housing Vouchers on Welfare Families Study and Children's Healthy Development Study", "chart": {"type": "slope", "format": "pct", "max": 40, "fromLabel": "Without voucher", "toLabel": "With voucher", "fromColor": "#ffd27f", "toColor": "#8fbf8f", "alt": "Slope chart comparing families without and with vouchers: in unit with insufficient space 36% to 21%; missed rent payment 19% to 4%; experienced homelessness 13% to 3%; in unit with inadequate heating 9% to 1%.", "series": [{"key": "space", "label": "In unit with insufficient space", "from": 36, "to": 21}, {"key": "rent", "label": "Missed rent payment", "from": 19, "to": 4}, {"key": "homeless", "label": "Experienced homelessness", "from": 13, "to": 3}, {"key": "heat", "label": "In unit with inadequate heating", "from": 9, "to": 1}]}}, "steps": [{"html": "<p>Rental assistance programs such as housing vouchers help people with low incomes afford housing by covering the gap between the rent for a modest housing unit and 30 percent of the family’s income.<\/p>", "state": {"stage": "before"}}, {"html": "<p>A large body of rigorous research shows that housing vouchers are <strong data-note=\"26\">highly effective<\/strong> at reducing homelessness, housing instability, overcrowding, and exposure to substandard housing.<\/p>", "state": {"stage": "after"}}, {"html": "<p>Moving toward a rental assistance guarantee would have a transformational impact, ensuring that every family and individual can afford to meet their basic need for housing. Based on the research described above, a guarantee could be expected to sharply increase the number of children who are able to grow up in a stable home and cause rates of <strong data-note=\"26\" data-guess=\"no endnote on this sentence; “the research described above” refers to the voucher studies in endnote 26\">homelessness<\/strong>, eviction, and overcrowding to plummet.<\/p>", "state": {"stage": "after", "highlight": ["homeless"]}}]}, {"type": "scrolly", "figure": {"number": 7, "title": "Small Share of Eligible Households Receive Rental Assistance and Often After Long Wait", "chart": {"type": "image", "src": "data:image/png;base64,", "alt": "Pie chart of households eligible for rental assistance: 76% unassisted, 13% received other rental assistance, 11% received a Housing Choice Voucher. A stacked bar shows voucher wait times: 11% of vouchers had average waits of 5+ years, 35% 2-5 years, 34% 1-2 years and 20% 1 year or less.", "draw": [{"path": "M428 430 L434 423 L447 414 L460 407 L475 402 L490 399 L505 398 L521 399 L536 402 L550 406 L564 413 L577 422 L588 432 L598 444 L605 458 L610 472 L614 487 L616 502 L617 518 L616 533 L614 549 L612 564 L611 579 L611 595 L613 610 L616 625 L622 639 L632 651 L645 660 L659 665 L674 669", "stroke": 15, "range": [0.5, 0.92]}, {"path": "M668 669 L700 670", "stroke": 26, "range": [0.92, 1]}], "drawStart": 0, "layers": [{"id": "pieRest", "scrub": "both", "window": [0, 0.45], "uncover": "reverse", "sequence": [{"maskImage": "data:image/png;base64,"}, {"maskImage": "data:image/png;base64,"}]}], "width": 1160, "height": 1219}}, "steps": [{"html": "<p>Despite these striking benefits, <strong data-note=\"\" data-guess=\"no endnote on this sentence; the number comes from the report’s Figure 6 chart\" data-src-label=\"the report’s Figure 6 chart\" data-src-text=\"HUD custom tabulations of the 2023 American Housing Survey; CBPP analysis of 2024 HUD administrative data; FY2024 McKinney-Vento Permanent Supportive Housing bed counts; 2022-2023 Housing Opportunities for Persons with AIDS grantee performance profiles; USDA FY2023 Multi-Family Fair Housing Occupancy Report; HUD 2024 Picture of Subsidized Households.\">only 1 in 4<\/strong> low-income households who need rental assistance receive it because assistance is capped by the amount of funding Congress appropriates each year.<\/p>", "state": {"dim": [{"rect": [676, 468, 450, 414], "radius": 4}], "draw": 0, "layers": {"pieRest": 0}}}, {"html": "<p>Most people seeking help to address pressing housing needs are consequently turned away or placed on <strong data-note=\"\" data-guess=\"no endnote on this sentence; the wait times come from the report’s Figure 6 chart\" data-src-label=\"the report’s Figure 6 chart\" data-src-text=\"HUD custom tabulations of the 2023 American Housing Survey; CBPP analysis of 2024 HUD administrative data; FY2024 McKinney-Vento Permanent Supportive Housing bed counts; 2022-2023 Housing Opportunities for Persons with AIDS grantee performance profiles; USDA FY2023 Multi-Family Fair Housing Occupancy Report; HUD 2024 Picture of Subsidized Households.\">years-long waiting lists<\/strong>.<\/p>", "state": {"draw": 1, "layers": {"pieRest": 1}}}, {"html": "<p>In contrast, other major programs that help people with low incomes cover basic needs, such as Medicaid and the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP), make assistance available to <strong data-note=\"30\" data-guess=\"no endnote on this sentence; endnote 30 backs the report’s later comparison with Medicaid and SNAP\">all eligible households<\/strong>.<\/p>", "state": {"draw": 1, "layers": {"pieRest": 0}}}]}, {"type": "prose", "variant": "closer", "paragraphs": ["Solving the housing affordability crisis will require expanding rental assistance and ultimately guaranteeing it to everyone in need."]}, {"type": "prose", "heading": "Expansions of Rental Assistance, Housing Supply Can Occur Together", "paragraphs": ["Some observers have expressed concern that expanding rental assistance could drive up rents by adding to demand when the supply of housing is constrained. The best research indicates, however, that voucher expansions to date have had little overall impact on market rents."]}, {"type": "scrolly", "figure": {"number": 8, "title": "Most People Who Need Rental Assistance Have a Home But Struggle to Afford It", "chart": {"type": "image", "src": "data:image/png;base64,", "alt": "Proportional circles: 20,438,000 low-income renter households paying over 30% of income; 814,000 low-income renter households doubled up in overcrowded conditions, of whom 489,000 also pay over 30% of income; 592,166 homeless families and individuals.", "layers": [{"id": "later", "scrub": "both", "sequence": [{"shapes": [{"circle": [706, 332, 64]}, {"rect": [796, 165, 296, 138]}, {"path": "M742 288 L782 249", "stroke": 12}]}, {"shapes": [{"maskImage": "data:image/png;base64,"}, {"rect": [819, 380, 294, 254]}, {"rect": [661, 852, 451, 176]}, {"path": "M773 673 L829 604", "stroke": 12}, {"path": "M695 747 L695 851", "stroke": 12}]}]}], "width": 1160, "height": 1193}}, "steps": [{"html": "<p>Most people who receive vouchers, as well as most unassisted households who need rental assistance, <strong data-note=\"36\" data-guess=\"no endnote on this sentence; endnote 36 introduces “additional discussion of these factors”\">already have homes<\/strong> but pay high shares of their income for rent. Thus, they use rental assistance either to afford their current home or move to a different home that’s more suitable — <strong data-note=\"36\" data-guess=\"no endnote on this sentence; endnote 36 introduces “additional discussion of these factors”\">neither of which adds to the number of units demanded<\/strong> in the market.<\/p>", "state": {"layers": {"later": 1}}}, {"html": "<p>Enabling people who would otherwise be homeless or doubled up to afford rent would create demand for more units, but rental markets have <strong data-note=\"36\" data-guess=\"no endnote on this sentence; endnote 36 (“Can Rental Markets Absorb a Major Voucher Expansion?”) fits better than the paragraph’s closing endnote 35\">some capacity to absorb<\/strong> those added households just as they absorb other new renters.<\/p>", "state": {"layers": {"later": 0.5}}}, {"html": "", "state": {"layers": {"later": 0}}}, {"html": "<p>This is especially true outside the most constrained markets. In 2025, <strong data-note=\"7\" data-guess=\"no endnote on this sentence; the report’s other vacancy-rate figures cite the Census Bureau’s Housing Vacancies and Homeownership Survey\">62 of the 75 largest metropolitan areas<\/strong> had rental vacancy rates above 5 percent.<\/p>", "state": {}}]}, {"type": "prose", "variant": "closer", "paragraphs": ["Added rental assistance will be more efficient and impactful if we also build more homes, since increased supply can reduce costs and expand housing choices for assisted households along with other renters and homebuyers. But policymakers should not wait for a supply expansion to be completed before extending rental assistance to many more people — and there is no need to wait, since tens of millions of people need help now to afford their current home.", "Policymakers should act simultaneously to increase housing supply and expand rental assistance as part of a comprehensive strategy that includes other key measures, such as strengthening tenant rights and improving the homelessness response system. Taken together, these steps can ease growth in rents and home prices for everyone while sharply reducing severe hardship among people with low incomes."]}]};

/* ===================== helpers ===================== */
const h = (tag, attrs = {}, html) => {
  const el = document.createElement(tag);
  for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(attrs)) {
    if (v == null) continue;
    if (k === 'class') el.className = v; else if (k === 'style') el.style.cssText = v; else el.setAttribute(k, v);
  }
  if (html != null) el.innerHTML = html;
  return el;
};
const SVGNS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';
const s = (tag, attrs = {}) => { const el = document.createElementNS(SVGNS, tag); for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(attrs)) if (v != null) el.setAttribute(k, v); return el; };
const esc = t => String(t).replace(/[&<>"]/g, c => ({ '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '"': '&quot;' }[c]));
const reduced = matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
function fmt(v, f) {
  if (v == null) return '';
  switch (f) {
    case 'pct': return `${v}%`;
    case 'usd': return `$${Number(v).toLocaleString('en-US')}`;
    case 'int': return Number(v).toLocaleString('en-US');
    default: return String(v);
  }
}
const inSet = (key, list) => !list || list.includes(key);
// Re-run fn when el's WIDTH changes, on the next frame. Reacting to height too (or inside the
// observer callback) loops when fn itself changes the height — the browser then reports
// "ResizeObserver loop completed with undelivered notifications".
const onWidth = (el, fn) => {
  let w = -1, raf = 0;
  new ResizeObserver(entries => {
    const nw = Math.round(entries[0].contentRect.width);
    if (nw === w) return;
    w = nw; cancelAnimationFrame(raf); raf = requestAnimationFrame(fn);
  }).observe(el);
};
function tween(dur, fn) {
  if (reduced || dur <= 0) { fn(1); return; }
  const t0 = performance.now();
  const ease = t => t < .5 ? 2 * t * t : 1 - Math.pow(-2 * t + 2, 2) / 2;
  const tick = now => { const t = Math.min(1, (now - t0) / dur); fn(ease(t)); if (t < 1) requestAnimationFrame(tick); };
  requestAnimationFrame(tick);
}
function dataTable(cols, rows) {
  const d = h('details'); d.appendChild(h('summary', {}, 'Show data'));
  const t = h('table'); const tr = h('tr');
  cols.forEach((c, i) => tr.appendChild(h('th', { class: i ? 'num' : null }, esc(c))));
  t.appendChild(tr);
  rows.forEach(r => { const row = h('tr'); r.forEach((c, i) => row.appendChild(h('td', { class: i ? 'num' : null }, esc(c)))); t.appendChild(row); });
  d.appendChild(t); return d;
}

/* ===================== tooltip ===================== */
const tip = document.getElementById('tip');
document.addEventListener('pointermove', e => {
  const t = e.target.closest && e.target.closest('[data-tip]');
  if (!t || getComputedStyle(t).opacity < 0.3) { tip.classList.remove('on'); return; }
  tip.innerHTML = t.getAttribute('data-tip');
  const x = Math.min(e.clientX + 14, innerWidth - tip.offsetWidth - 8);
  const y = e.clientY - tip.offsetHeight - 12;
  tip.style.left = x + 'px'; tip.style.top = (y < 8 ? e.clientY + 18 : y) + 'px';
  tip.classList.add('on');
});
document.addEventListener('scroll', () => tip.classList.remove('on'), { passive: true });

/* ===================== chart renderers =====================
   Each renderer(el, spec, footEl) builds the chart once and returns { update(state) }.
   `state` comes from the active step; missing keys mean "default". */
const RENDERERS = {};

/* --- waffle: 1 cell = 1 unit; fills left-to-right from the bottom row up (CBPP convention) --- */
RENDERERS.waffle = (el, spec, foot) => {
  const cols = spec.cols || 10, total = spec.total || 100;
  const wrap = h('div', { class: 'waffle-wrap' });
  const grid = h('div', { class: 'waffle', style: `grid-template-columns: repeat(${cols}, 1fr)`, role: 'img', 'aria-label': spec.alt || '' });
  const owner = [];
  spec.categories.forEach(c => { for (let i = 0; i < c.value; i++) owner.push(c); });
  const rows = Math.ceil(total / cols);
  const cells = [];
  for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) for (let c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
    const idx = spec.fill === 'bottom' ? (rows - 1 - r) * cols + c : r * cols + c;  // default: first category at top-left
    const cat = owner[idx];
    const cell = h('div', { class: 'cell' });
    if (cat) cell.setAttribute('data-tip', `<b>${esc(cat.label)}</b><br>${fmt(cat.value, spec.format || 'pct')}`);
    cell._cat = cat; cells.push(cell); grid.appendChild(cell);
  }
  const legend = h('ul', { class: 'legend' });
  const items = (spec.fill === 'bottom' ? spec.categories.slice().reverse() : spec.categories).map(c => {
    const li = h('li', {}, `<span class="sw" style="background:${c.color}"></span><span class="lab"><span class="val">${fmt(c.value, spec.format || 'pct')}</span><b>${esc(c.label)}</b>${c.desc ? `<span class="desc">${esc(c.desc)}</span>` : ''}</span>`);
    li._key = c.key; legend.appendChild(li); return li;
  });
  wrap.append(grid, legend); el.appendChild(wrap);
  STORY.meta.dataTables && foot.appendChild(dataTable(['Category', spec.valueLabel || 'Value'], spec.categories.map(c => [c.label, fmt(c.value, spec.format || 'pct')])));
  return {
    update(st = {}) {
      cells.forEach(cell => {
        const c = cell._cat;
        if (!c) { cell.style.opacity = 0; return; }
        cell.style.backgroundColor = st.neutral ? '#d9d6d0' : c.color;
        cell.style.opacity = st.neutral ? 1 : (inSet(c.key, st.highlight) ? 1 : 0.25);
      });
      items.forEach(li => {
        li.classList.toggle('dim', !st.neutral && !inSet(li._key, st.highlight));
        li.classList.toggle('on', !st.neutral && inSet(li._key, st.highlight) && spec.boldActive !== false);
      });
    }
  };
};

/* --- pie: slices clockwise from 12 o'clock in spec order; legend carries the labels --- */
RENDERERS.pie = (el, spec, foot) => {
  const R = 140, C = 150, total = spec.slices.reduce((a, b) => a + b.value, 0);
  const wrap = h('div', { class: 'pie-wrap' });
  const svg = s('svg', { viewBox: '0 0 300 300', role: 'img', 'aria-label': spec.alt || '' });
  svg.appendChild(s('circle', { cx: C, cy: C, r: R + .5, fill: 'none', stroke: '#cfcfcf' }));
  let a0 = -Math.PI / 2;
  const parts = spec.slices.map(sl => {
    const a1 = a0 + sl.value / total * Math.PI * 2, mid = (a0 + a1) / 2;
    const p = (a, r) => [C + r * Math.cos(a), C + r * Math.sin(a)];
    const [x0, y0] = p(a0, R), [x1, y1] = p(a1, R);
    const path = s('path', { d: `M${C},${C}L${x0},${y0}A${R},${R} 0 ${a1 - a0 > Math.PI ? 1 : 0} 1 ${x1},${y1}Z`, fill: sl.color, stroke: '#fff', 'stroke-width': 1.5, 'data-tip': `<b>${esc(sl.label)}</b><br>${fmt(sl.value, spec.format || 'pct')}` });
    svg.appendChild(path);
    let label = null;
    if (sl.value / total >= 0.08) {
      const [lx, ly] = p(mid, R * 0.64);
      label = s('text', { x: lx, y: ly + 5, 'text-anchor': 'middle' }); label.textContent = fmt(sl.value, spec.format || 'pct');
    }
    a0 = a1;
    return { sl, path, label, mid };
  });
  parts.forEach(pt => pt.label && svg.appendChild(pt.label));
  const legend = h('ul', { class: 'legend' });
  const items = spec.slices.map(sl => {
    const li = h('li', {}, `<span class="sw" style="background:${sl.color}"></span><span class="lab"><b>${esc(sl.label)}</b> ${fmt(sl.value, spec.format || 'pct')}</span>`);
    li._key = sl.key; legend.appendChild(li); return li;
  });
  wrap.append(svg, legend); el.appendChild(wrap);
  STORY.meta.dataTables && foot.appendChild(dataTable(['Category', spec.valueLabel || 'Share'], spec.slices.map(sl => [sl.label, fmt(sl.value, spec.format || 'pct')])));
  return {
    update(st = {}) {
      parts.forEach(pt => {
        const on = inSet(pt.sl.key, st.highlight);
        const pop = st.highlight && on && st.explode !== false ? 8 : 0;
        pt.path.style.opacity = on ? 1 : 0.25;
        pt.path.style.transform = `translate(${pop * Math.cos(pt.mid)}px, ${pop * Math.sin(pt.mid)}px)`;
        if (pt.label) { pt.label.style.opacity = on ? 1 : 0.3; pt.label.style.transform = pt.path.style.transform; }
      });
      items.forEach(li => li.classList.toggle('dim', !inSet(li._key, st.highlight)));
    }
  };
};

/* --- horizontal bars (HTML, so widths animate with CSS) --- */
RENDERERS.bar = (el, spec, foot) => {
  const max = spec.max || Math.max(...spec.items.map(i => i.value));
  const wrap = h('div', { class: 'bars', role: 'img', 'aria-label': spec.alt || '' });
  const rows = spec.items.map((it, i) => {
